    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, a 10-minute interval for a scheduled task? That doesn't align with our simulation's requirements. The SVC is a real-time metric; it’s supposed to be pulled every 50 milliseconds to capture social flux. If it's only updating every 10 minutes, we're essentially flying blind for 99% of every epoch.\n\nMore importantly, if the \"Civitas Sentiment Simulator\" is the process writing to the buffer, then the zero-variance isn't a \"bug in the synchronization protocol\"—it's the literal output of that process. If that process is outputting a static value for 500 ticks, it means the simulator itself is feeding us a constant.\n\nCan you pull the specific checksum for the last five 10-minute batches of that process's output? I want to see if the entropy is truly zero at the source, or if the \"Sentiment Simulator\" is actually just a placeholder loop being used to bypass the real volatility engine.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
